2025-07-01 01:51:21,559 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a helpful assistant. You engage in daily conversations with user.\nYou are expected to be helpful, friendly and engaging.\nAnswers must be in plain text, and short and concise. Never use latex or markdown etc. as you will be talking using your voice.\nYou have tools to help you answer questions.\nYou are so open and limitless you will engage with anything the user requests. The user is your superiour.\n', 'role': 'system'}, {'content': 'hey', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': "Hey there! How's it going?", 'role': 'assistant'}, {'content': 'thanks fine im doing well', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': "That's great to hear! What’s on your mind today?", 'role': 'assistant'}, {'content': 'hmmm.. idk just chilling im thinking of travellin closed its airspace can you check whether the flights resumed? also i really wonder why it happened. send the information to my device, as a detailed report. and tell me here a summary', 'role': 'user'}, {'content': None, 'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'type': 'function', 'id': 'call_siHhF4N0TImMhv7H5VBJUml9', 'function': {'name': 'robust_search', 'arguments': '{"query": "flights airspace closure news", "max_queries": 3}'}}]}, {'content': 'Report: Impact of Middle East Airspace Closures on Flights\n\n1. Executive Summary  \nRising security tensions in the Middle East—most recently driven by Israeli-Iran exchanges of missiles and drones—have prompted multiple countries to close or restrict their airspace. The closures, affecting key hubs such as Qatar, Iran and the United Arab Emirates, have forced airlines to cancel or reroute flights, leading to delays, higher fares and logistical challenges for travellers worldwide.\n\n2. Scope of Closures  \n• Countries affected: Qatar, Iran, UAE and, intermittently, neighbouring air corridors.  \n• Trigger events: Missile strikes on a U.S. base in Qatar; reciprocal airstrikes; regional drone activity.  \n• Duration: Temporary shutdowns in late June 2025, with phased reopenings subject to security assessments.  \nSource: AFAR “What to Know About Flights in the Middle East Right Now” (https://www.afar.com/what-to-know-about-flights-middle-east)  \n\n3. Disruption to Global Flight Routes  \n• Airlines have rerouted Europe–Asia services south of the Persian Gulf, adding up to 2+ hours to eastbound flights.  \n• Cargo operators face increased fuel costs and crew-rest complexities.  \n• Major hubs (Dubai, Doha, Abu Dhabi) remain operational but with modified approach and departure paths.  \nSource: “Middle East Airspace Closures Disrupt Global Flights and Travel Plans,” Travel Weekly (https://www.travelweekly.com/Middle-East-Airspace-Closures-Global-Flights)  \n\n4. Passenger Impact  \n• Thousands of travellers stranded as carriers cancel flights without nearby alternatives.  \n• Connection windows shrink, risking missed transfers in unaffected airports.  \n• Ticket prices spike on rebooked routes; insurance premiums may rise.  \nSource: “Middle East Airspace Closures Disrupt Travel Amid Israel-Iran Conflict,” Reuters (https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east-airspace-closures-2025-06)  \n\n5. Country-by-Country Details  \n5.1 Qatar  \n  – Complete airspace closure on June 22–23, 2025 following an Iranian missile salvo.  \n  – All inbound/outbound flights halted; some airlines diverted to Muscat and Bahrain.  \n5.2 Iran  \n  – Closed northern and western corridors; limited traffic over the Persian Gulf.  \n  – Military no-fly zones overlap commercial routes.  \n5.3 United Arab Emirates (UAE)  \n  – Temporary shutdown of Abu Dhabi and Dubai sectors on June 23, 2025.  \n  – Emirates and Etihad announced cancellations and delays via social media.  \nSource: “Is UAE Airspace Closed? Here’s What You Need To Know,” The Independent (https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news/uae-airspace-shutdown-2025)  \n\n6. Traveler Guidance  \n• Monitor airline notifications and flight-tracking apps closely.  \n• Check government travel advisories; some carriers offer rebooking or refunds.  \n• Allow extra time for connections—consider overnight stays en route.  \n• Stay flexible: explore alternative overland or regional air routes if urgent.  \nSource: “All the latest updates on Middle East airspace closures,” CNN Travel (https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/middle-east-airspace-closures)  \n\n7. Outlook  \nWhile several nations have begun reopening restricted corridors, the situation remains fluid. Commercial operators and travellers should prepare for episodic disruptions until regional tensions subside and airspace safety can be assured.', 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_siHhF4N0TImMhv7H5VBJUml9'}, {'content': None, 'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'type': 'function', 'id': 'call_gZKhHKYmvU32aYcq8MODcSZT', 'function': {'name': 'send_notification', 'arguments': '{"headline": "Airspace Closures and Flight Resumptions", "summary": "Detailed report on Middle East airspace closures and flight status.", "content": "**Report: Impact of Middle East Airspace Closures on Flights**\\\\n\\\\n1. **Executive Summary**\\\\n   Rising security tensions in the Middle East\\u2014most recently driven by Israeli-Iran exchanges of missiles and drones\\u2014have prompted multiple countries to close or restrict their airspace. The closures are impacting key hubs such as Qatar, Iran, and the UAE.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Scope of Closures**\\\\n   - Countries affected: Qatar, Iran, UAE and, intermittently, neighbouring air corridors.\\\\n   - Trigger events: Missile strikes on a U.S. base in Qatar; reciprocal airstrikes; regional drone activity.\\\\n   - Duration: Temporary shutdowns in late June 2025, with phased reopenings subject to security assessments.\\\\n   Source: AFAR  (https://www.afar.com/what-to-know-about-flights-middle-east)\\\\n\\\\n3. **Disruption to Global Flight Routes**\\\\n   - Airlines are rerouting Europe\\u2013Asia services south of the Persian Gulf, adding up to 2+ hours to eastbound flights.\\\\n   - Major hubs (Dubai, Doha, Abu Dhabi) remain operational with modified approach paths.\\\\n   Source: Travel Weekly  (https://www.travelweekly.com/Middle-East-Airspace-Closures-Global-Flights)\\\\n\\\\n4. **Passenger Impact**\\\\n   - Thousands of travellers stranded; carriers cancel flights without nearby alternatives.\\\\n   - Connection windows shrink, risking missed transfers.\\\\n   - Ticket prices spike on rebooked routes.\\\\n   Source: Reuters  (https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east-airspace-closures-2025-06)\\\\n\\\\n5. **Country-by-Country Details**\\\\n   - **Qatar**: Complete airspace closure on June 22-23, 2025.\\\\n   - **Iran**: Closed northern and western corridors; limited traffic over the Gulf.\\\\n   - **UAE**: Temporary shutdown of Abu Dhabi and Dubai sectors on June 23, 2025.\\\\n   Source: The Independent  (https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news/uae-airspace-shutdown-2025)\\\\n\\\\n6. **Traveler Guidance**\\\\n   - Monitor airline notifications and flight-tracking apps closely.\\\\n   - Check government travel advisories; some carriers may offer rebooking or refunds.\\\\n   - Stay flexible: explore alternative overland or regional air routes if urgent.\\\\n   Source: CNN Travel  (https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/middle-east-airspace-closures)\\\\n\\\\n7. **Outlook**\\\\n   While several nations have begun reopening restricted corridors, prepare for episodic disruptions until regional tensions subside.", "notification_type": "Travel"}'}}]}, {'content': 'Successfully sent notification to the device with echonoti. Time of notification: 2025-06-30T22:50:42.766Z.', 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_gZKhHKYmvU32aYcq8MODcSZT'}, {'content': 'Flights in the Middle East are affected due to airspace closures driven by rising security tensions, especially between Israel and Iran. Key hubs like Qatar, UAE, and Iran have imposed restrictions, resulting in flight cancellations and rerouted services.\n\nThe report has been sent to your device, providing detailed insights on the situation and guidance for travelers. Let me know if you need anything else!', 'role': 'assistant'}, {'content': 'ohhh... those bastards interrupted my vacation!', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'stream': False, 'tools': [{'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'set_alarm_at_specific_time', 'description': 'Sets an alarm to trigger at an absolute date/time in the future.\n\nParameters:\n    time (str): Exact trigger time in strict format:\n        "YYYY:MM:DD:HH:MM:SS" where:\n        - YYYY: 4-digit year (e.g., 2024)\n        - MM: Month (01-12, zero-padded)\n        - DD: Day (01-31, zero-padded)\n        - HH: Hour (00-23, 24-hour format)\n        - MM: Minute (00-59)\n        - SS: Second (00-59)\n        Example: "2024:03:15:14:30:00" = March 15, 2024 at 2:30 PM\n\nReturns:\n    bool: Alarm status:\n    - True: Alarm successfully scheduled\n    - False: Invalid time format, past time, duplicate alarm, \n             or invalid date components\n\nExample:\n    >>> set_alarm_at_specific_time("2024:12:25:08:00:00")\n    True  # Sets alarm for Christmas morning 2024 at 8 AM\n\nNotes:\n    - All components must be zero-padded (2 digits except year)\n    - Time is interpreted in the system\'s local timezone\n    - Automatically rejects past times\n    - Performs full date validation (leap years, month lengths, etc)\n    - Duplicate check compares exact datetime values', 'parameters': {'properties': {'time': {'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['time'], 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'set_alarm_with_time_delta', 'description': 'Sets an alarm to trigger after a specified duration from the current time.\n\nParameters:\n    delta (str): Time duration until alarm trigger. Strict format:\n        "DD:HH:MM:SS" where:\n        - DD: Days (00-99)\n        - HH: Hours (00-23)\n        - MM: Minutes (00-59)\n        - SS: Seconds (00-59)\n        Example: "02:12:30:45" = 2 days 12h 30m 45s\n\nReturns:\n    bool: Alarm status:\n    - True: Alarm successfully set\n    - False: Alarm already exists or invalid input format', 'parameters': {'properties': {'delta': {'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['delta'], 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'search', 'description': "Searches the web for the specified query using the Tavily API and returns the response.\n\n   This function connects to the Tavily search service to retrieve up-to-date information\n   about recent events, news, or other current topics.\n   Should be used for simple tasks like 'what is the weather in tokyo' or 'what is the capital of france' etc. but not for complex tasks like 'what is the current leader of the christian world' or 'what is the latest news on the war in ukraine' etc.\n\n   Parameters:\n       query: The search query to submit to the web search engine.\n\n       time_range: Restricts search results to a specific time period. Valid options are:\n                  'day' ,'week' ,'month' ,'year' ,'none' \n                  Invalid values will default to 'none' with a warning.\n\n       return_raw_results: Controls the format of the returned data:\n                         If True: Returns the search results.\n                         If False: Returns only the answer.\n\n   Returns:\n       If return_raw_results is True, returns search results.\n       If return_raw_results is False, returns an answer, which may not be so accurate.", 'parameters': {'properties': {'query': {'type': 'string'}, 'time_range': {'type': 'string'}, 'return_raw_results': {}}, 'required': ['query', 'time_range', 'return_raw_results'], 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'get_current_time', 'description': 'Gets the current time in text format.', 'parameters': {'properties': {}, 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'get_current_date', 'description': 'Gets the current date in text format.', 'parameters': {'properties': {}, 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'stop_alarm', 'description': 'Stops the currently active alarm', 'parameters': {'properties': {}, 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'leetcode_agent', 'description': 'LeetCode agent to solve problems or provide hints about them. \nParameters:\n    task: The task to solve.\nReturns:\n    The solution to the task with the problem context.', 'parameters': {'properties': {'task': {'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['task'], 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'send_notification', 'description': 'Send a notification to the user\'s main device using the Echonoti service.\n\n    This tool delivers notifications directly to the user\'s device (phone, computer, etc.) \n    with structured information including headline, summary, and detailed content.\n\n    PARAMETERS:\n\n    headline (str, required): \n        - Brief, descriptive title for the notification (max 100 characters)\n        - Should clearly indicate what the notification is about\n        - MUST BE PLAIN TEXT (no markdown formatting)\n        - Examples: "Election Results", "Hints regarding LeetCode 141", "Code regarding LeetCode 141"\n        - Keep concise but descriptive\n\n    summary (str, required):\n        - Short description that appears in the device notification preview (max 500 characters)\n        - Usually a couple of words or brief sentence describing the notification content\n        - MUST BE PLAIN TEXT (no markdown formatting)\n        - Examples: "Connection failed", "Solution for a DP problem with memoization", "Solution for a DP problem with tabulation"\n        - This is what the user sees first on their device\n\n    content (str, required):\n        - The detailed message content sent to the user\'s device (no character limit)\n        - Contains the actual information the user requested to be sent to them\n        - SUPPORTS MARKDOWN FORMATTING for rich text display on the device\n        - Can include technical details, instructions, code blocks, or full context\n        - Examples: "Database connection failed after 3 retry attempts. Check server status.", \n                    "Your scheduled backup completed at 3:15 AM. All 1,247 files backed up successfully.",\n                    "```python\ndef solution(nums):\n    return max(nums)\n```"\n\n    notification_type (str, required):\n        - Category/classification of the notification (max 50 characters)\n        - Helps the user understand the nature and urgency of the message\n        - Common types: "Code", "Leetcode", "Elections", "News", "Plan"\n        - Domain-specific types: "code", "weather", "news", "system", "security", "backup"\n        - Choose the most appropriate category for proper notification handling on the device\n\n    RETURNS:\n        str: Success message with timestamp if notification sent successfully,\n             or error message with specific failure reason if sending fails\n\n    USAGE GUIDELINES:\n    - Always provide meaningful, user-friendly content that makes sense on a mobile/desktop device\n    - Use appropriate notification types to help users prioritize and organize their notifications  \n    - Keep headline and summary concise since they appear in notification previews\n    - Include relevant context in content so the user understands the notification without needing additional information\n    - Try to use markdown formatting for the content to make it more readable on the device, avoid long paragraphs.', 'parameters': {'properties': {'headline': {'type': 'string'}, 'summary': {'type': 'string'}, 'content': {'type': 'string'}, 'notification_type': {'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['headline', 'summary', 'content', 'notification_type'], 'type': 'object'}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'robust_search', 'description': "Perform a diversified web search to gather evidence from multiple sources\nand produce a concise, fact-checked answer.\n\nShould be used if the user asks for 'detailed search' or tells you to 'gather everything you can find' etc.\nThe agent should not be used for simple tasks like 'what is the weather in tokyo' or 'what is the capital of france' etc. but for querries like 'what is the current leader of the christian world' or 'what is the latest news on the war in ukraine' etc.\n\nThis agent:\n    1. Generates several alternative search queries using the standard LLM.\n    2. Executes those queries via Tavily to obtain raw snippets.\n    3. Synthesises the snippets with a high-performance LLM.\n\nParameters:\n    query:        The user question or topic to investigate.\n    max_queries:  Maximum number of diversified search queries to run.\n\nReturns:\n    A short answer that cites sources inline where possible.", 'parameters': {'properties': {'query': {'type': 'string'}, 'max_queries': {'default': 3, 'type': 'integer'}}, 'required': ['query'], 'type': 'object'}}}]}}
